D5's drift guard cannot run in CI — and the mirror had already drifted

**Corrects #236, which I got wrong the same day, and the truth is a better finding.**

#236 said the `age` copies had *"nothing that fails when they drift"* and that
`composer/` was the source for all of it. Both wrong. There **is** a drift guard,
the direction is per-file and runs both ways, and the mirroring is a documented
discipline rather than an improvisation — `docs/decisions.md` **D5 · Mirror
discipline**, accepted 2026-07.

| module | source of truth | mirror |
|---|---|---|
| `chacha20poly1305.mjs` | `composer/` | `data-pile/bin/` |
| `age-open.mjs` | **`data-pile/bin/`** | `composer/` |
| `feed-open.mjs` | **`data-pile/bin/`** | `composer/` |
| `age-keygen.mjs` | `composer/age-mint.mjs` | `data-pile/bin/`, a *trimmed slice* — D5's one allowed exception |

Everything #236 concluded about `age-enough` still stands: no rival implementations,
so no merge, and the five `apt-get install age` steps break down exactly as stated.
What changes is the reason it matters.

## The guard resolves its subject by hoping

`composer/pile-keeper.test.mjs` asserts the mirrors are byte-identical to
`data-pile/bin/`. It works. It also cannot run in CI:

    const dp = process.env.DP_REPO || join(root, "..", "data-pile");
    if (existsSync(join(dp, "bin", "feed-open.mjs"))) { …assert… }
    else console.log("  note: mirror drift guard skipped (no data-pile checkout; set DP_REPO)");

A **sibling checkout on disk.** `actions/checkout` gives a job one repository,
`.gitmodules` names only `jekyll-enough`, and no workflow sets `DP_REPO`. So in CI the
`existsSync` is false, the guard prints a note, and the suite passes. It holds only
where both repos happen to sit side by side — which is to say it holds by coincidence.
It also skipped in the worktree I wrote this in, for the same reason.

## It had already failed, and nobody had seen it

    $ node composer/pile-keeper.test.mjs
    FAIL: composer/feed-open.mjs is byte-identical to data-pile/bin/feed-open.mjs
      ok: composer/age-open.mjs is byte-identical to data-pile/bin/age-open.mjs

**39 lines.** `data-pile/bin/feed-open.mjs` grew a `partial: true` verification mode —
for *"a reader that holds only SOME blocks: a disclosed excerpt, an ejected piece, a
player that has fetched eight chunks of ninety-three"* — and `composer/`'s mirror did
not have it. The half that most needs partial verification is the browser half, and the
browser half is the one that missed it.

D5's stated reason is *"avoids two copies drifting into two behaviors — **especially
dangerous for crypto/verify code**."* `feed-open.mjs` is verify code. The discipline was
right and the guard was written, and it still happened, because the guard was
conditional on something CI does not have.

## Fixed here, and one thing deliberately not

`composer/feed-open.mjs` is **re-copied verbatim** from the source of truth, which is
what D5 says to do when the source moves. Its only consumer,
`composer/pile-keeper.mjs:142`, passes no `partial` and the new parameter defaults to
`false` — behaviour unchanged, and the browser half now has the mode it was missing.

    $ DP_REPO=~/Project/FCCN-ANTIBODY/data-pile node scripts/test.mjs
      ok: composer/feed-open.mjs is byte-identical to data-pile/bin/feed-open.mjs
      ok: composer/age-open.mjs is byte-identical to data-pile/bin/age-open.mjs
      118/118 suites passed

**Not fixed: the guard's reach**, and I did not want to paper over it from this side.
Making the skip fatal would fail every CI run, because CI genuinely has no `data-pile`.
The fix is the address — a submodule pin cannot drift — which is the extraction work
already in flight. Until then this can recur at any time, and the only thing between it
and production is whether someone runs the suite on a workstation with both repos
checked out.

## The pattern, which is the part worth keeping

This is the same shape as #233's submodule guard: **a check that silently becomes a
no-op when what it needs is not on disk, reporting a pass for work it did not do.** Two
instances in two days, found independently, in a codebase that is otherwise careful
about exactly this.

> A guard that can skip is a guard that will skip, and the skip is the state nobody
> reads.

Worth a sweep of its own for `if (existsSync(...)) { assert }` — I have not done one.
Flagging rather than doing, since you have agents on extractions and this is adjacent
to all of them.

## On framing

#236 called the vendoring *"the failure the extraction exists to prevent, already
committed."* That was the wrong register — you've since said `composer/` is a quarry
and the looting is on purpose, and D5 shows it was deliberate and documented all along.
The mirroring is not the defect. The unenforceable guard is, and the missing address is
why the guard had to exist.
